claimed: channel-laws — the operator's 1+2, after ring-channel withdrew two implementations that each rediscovered the same invariants by failing.

(1) LAWS. The Channel interface states operations but not invariants, so every new implementation must rediscover them from its own bugs -- which is exactly what happened four times. Write the contract as a shared suite parameterised over the implementation (the project's existing convention: munit.ScalaCheckSuite, `property(...)`, TestLaws.scala), and run StmChannel against it as the reference. A future implementation then earns its place by passing the laws, not by surviving a gate three times.

(2) NARROW THE CLOSE PRIMITIVE. Verified from zio sources: ZIO's Queue.shutdown INTERRUPTS offers and takes and promises no drain, while our close promises buffered elements still drain -- a strictly harder contract, and the one all four bugs lived in. The hard part is termination detection: "no more sends will be accepted AND the buffer is empty" is one atomic fact in StmChannel and a distributed one once elements leave the state. Narrow it by making the OBSERVABLE closed state two-phase: refuse new sends first, wait for accepted-but-unbuffered sends, and only then publish the flag consumers terminate on. Then a consumer's end condition is just closed-and-empty, and implementations stop having to invent the barrier themselves.
